<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/advanced/translation-versus-interpretation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    C1 fluency is the doorway to the language professions, and its two great crafts share a name and little
    else. <strong>Translation</strong> is written: a craft of time, research and revision, judged on the page
    years later. <strong>Interpretation</strong> is live: a cognitive performance under real-time pressure,
    judged in seconds and unrecoverable. Both face the same wall — languages do not map one-to-one — and both
    have developed explicit, teachable methods for deciding <em>what to sacrifice and what to keep</em>. This
    lesson builds that decision toolkit, then adds the force that reshaped the profession: machine
    translation.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the craft, the performance, the untranslatables, and the machine" widget="equationStep" config={transCfg} />

  <h2>The equivalence question: what are you translating?</h2>
  <p>
    The founding debate asks what a translation should be equivalent <em>to</em>. Eugene Nida's classic
    distinction: <strong>formal equivalence</strong> stays close to source form and structure;
    <strong>dynamic (functional) equivalence</strong> reproduces the source's <em>effect</em> on its readers.
    They can genuinely conflict: the Bible translator Nida served chose dynamic rendering so that every
    congregation meets a living text, while a legal translation must be formally exact even where the
    result reads stiffly. Hans Vermeer's <strong>Skopos theory</strong> (1978) generalised the insight: the
    translation's <strong>purpose</strong> — its skopos — decides the strategy, so there is no single valid
    output of a source text, only outputs valid for a purpose. Worked example, one source sentence, three
    skopoi. German: "Der Bericht muss bis Freitag vorliegen." A legal rendering: "The report must be
    submitted by Friday" (obligation preserved exactly). A business email: "Could you make sure I have the
    report by Friday?" (purpose: get the report; register: polite request). A novel, spoken by a general:
    "Friday. The report. No exceptions." (effect: command). Three faithful translations of one sentence —
    the translator's first skill is asking <em>what this translation is for</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Domestication versus foreignisation</h2>
  <p>
    Lawrence Venuti's influential distinction names the culture-level choice: <strong>domestication</strong>
    smooths the text into the target culture's conventions (translate the Japanese -san as Mr or Ms; rename
    foods; normalise word order), rendering the translator invisible; <strong>foreignisation</strong> keeps
    the source's strangeness visible (keep sensei, honorifics, syntax rhythms), reminding readers they are
    reading a translation from elsewhere. Neither is correct: a global thriller probably wants domestication;
    a scholarly edition of <em>The Tale of Genji</em> may want heavy foreignisation; a manga series lives
    from the tension between the two. Venuti's polemical point is that the choice is ideological —
    domestication quietly makes the other culture fluent in ours — which is why literary translation is a
    place where these decisions are debated openly. Related is the localisation layer for products:
    date formats, currencies, colour connotations, jokes and imagery are adapted wholesale, which is why
    global software is "localised," not merely translated.
  </p>

  <h2>The untranslatable inventory — and the five escapes</h2>
  <p>
    Some content resists transfer absolutely. <strong>Lexical gaps</strong>: Portuguese <em>saudade</em>,
    Danish <em>hygge</em>, Japanese <em>amae</em>, German <em>Schadenfreude</em> name packages of meaning
    other languages store in sentences (with the standard caveat: the gap is one of one-word equivalents,
    not of meaning — English can circumscribe any of them, less compactly). <strong>Untranslatable
    forms</strong>: puns play on one language's sound shape (a pun on "cloth" dies on the page in Japanese);
    poetry binds sound to sense; dialect marks class and region with no target equivalent. <strong>System
    gaps</strong>: honorific systems, gendered grammars, evidentiality (some languages grammatically mark
    how you know something). The professional's five escapes, in rising order of intervention:
    <strong>borrowing</strong> (import the word — schadenfreude, saudade have entered English),
    <strong>calque</strong> (translate the parts — "flea market" from French <em>marché aux puces</em>),
    <strong>substitution</strong> (a target-culture equivalent effect — replace the untranslatable pun with
    a different pun), <strong>explanation</strong> (keep + gloss), and <strong>omission or compensation</strong>
    (drop it here, add wordplay elsewhere). Every choice loses something; the craft is choosing
    <em>deliberately</em> what to lose, and being able to defend the choice.
  </p>

  <h2>Interpretation: the cognitive performance</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Consecutive interpretation</strong> — speaker talks for minutes, then pauses while you render —
    is built on a note-taking system of its own (Rozan's method: verticality, abbreviations, links between
    ideas) plus trained memory for sense, not words. <strong>Simultaneous interpretation</strong> — live,
    through a headset, with an ear-voice span of roughly 2–4 seconds — is one of the most extreme routine
    cognitive loads in any profession: Daniel Gile's effort model decomposes it into concurrent
    <strong>listening and analysis, memory, and production</strong>, three processes sharing one working
    memory, which is why interpreters <strong>work in 20–30-minute shifts in pairs</strong>, why numbers and
    idioms cause the classic failures (they are long, low-redundancy chunks that cannot be paraphrased
    safely), and why debriefing discipline ("say the meaning, never improvise facts") is drilled.
    Anticipation, compression ("what was said at greater length was:...") and name-and-number strategies
    are the performance's technique. <strong>Sight translation</strong> (reading a document aloud in the
    other language) sits between the crafts and is used more than either beginners expect.
  </p>

  <h2>The machine and the modern trade</h2>
  <p>
    Neural machine translation (from 2016's transformer-era systems onward) moved routine translation from
    "word salad" to serviceable in seconds for high-resource pairs. The professional map of what it did:
    <strong>routine, high-volume, low-stakes text</strong> (product listings, internal documentation,
    rough drafts) is now machine-translated and <strong>post-edited</strong> (MTPE) by humans;
    <strong>high-stakes and high-register text</strong> — contracts, patents, medical records, literature,
    marketing — remains human-led, because machines fail exactly where professionals add value: register
    consistency, culture-bound reference, implicit meaning, and <strong>accountability</strong> (a mistranslated
    dosage has a liable human name on it). Literary MT remains reliably tone-deaf — fluent sentences,
    wrong book. The craft implication for the C1 learner: the commodity floor has risen; what commands work
    is domain expertise, judgement, revision quality, and the interpersonal trust machines do not carry.
  </p>

  <h2>One poem, two renderings: what each choice costs</h2>
  <p>
    The untranslatable inventory becomes vivid in miniature. Bashō's most famous haiku: <em>furuike ya
    / kawazu tobikomu / mizu no oto</em> — old pond / frog jumps-in / water's sound. A literal rendering
    ("The old pond — / a frog leaps in, / the sound of water") keeps the thirteen-beat structure, the
    cutting word <em>ya</em>'s pause, and the poem's astonishing event: sound arriving as if the world
    had been silent until the frog. A domesticated rendering adds interpretation ("stillness — the frog
    startles the pond awake") and gains immediacy while losing the objectivity that makes the original
    feel discovered rather than composed. Both are defensible; each sacrifices differently — the first
    risks flatness for fidelity, the second risks paraphrase for effect. Scale the same choices up and
    you have literary translation's whole economy: rhythm versus sense, strangeness versus fluency, the
    poem the author wrote versus the poem the reader would have had. The craft rule that falls out: the
    translator chooses a <em>priority order</em> for each text (image, rhythm, register, form) and
    defends it consistently — the sin is not any single loss, but unexamined, shifting losses.
  </p>

  <h2>Quality, workflow, and the interpreter's oath</h2>
  <p>
    Professional quality is managed, not inspired. The workflow runs <strong>brief → draft → revision →
    proof</strong>: the brief (audience, purpose, style guide, glossary — the skopos made operational)
    precedes everything; the draft is for meaning; revision is for the target language (professionals
    self-revise in a separate pass, ideally after time away — the same advice this ladder's writing
    lessons give, for the same cognitive reason); proof is mechanical (numbers, names, tags, formatting).
    Error taxonomies separate <strong>accuracy</strong> (mistranslation, omission, addition),
    <strong>terminology</strong> (wrong domain term), and <strong>register/style</strong> — and training
    means scoring your own work against the taxonomy, not just "rereading it." One caution: back-translation
    (translate to A, machine-translate back to B) is a diagnostic, not a quality measure — it flags
    ambiguities and punishes legitimate restructuring. Interpreters carry an <strong>oath</strong>-level
    ethics instead: strict confidentiality (court and medical interpreters are bound like lawyers),
    strict neutrality (render everything, add nothing, soften nothing — including profanity and
    insults), and first-person delivery ("I" means the speaker, not "he says he") — because the room's
    decisions depend on the words arriving undistorted. The modern professional, in short, pairs the
    machine's throughput with the oath's judgement.
  </p>

  <h2>The decision tree, formalised: a translation brief in action</h2>
  <p>
    Put the whole toolkit in order with one professional scenario. A client sends a 4,000-word
    sustainability report from a German manufacturer, bound for British investors. The brief you write
    back (and should insist on): <strong>audience</strong> — finance-literate readers who will not know
    German industrial vocabulary; <strong>purpose</strong> — disclosure document, so precision outranks
    elegance, but register must be formal British business English; <strong>glossary</strong> — agreed
    renderings for recurring terms (Wertberichtigung, Mittelstand, Energiewende — borrow, explain, or
    translate? decide once, apply everywhere); <strong>references</strong> — the previous year's report,
    so continuity survives personnel changes. Now the execution decisions the lesson's machinery drives:
    skopos says disclosure register, so dynamic equivalence is capped — you may restructure sentences,
    never numbers or commitments; domestication governs formats (decimal commas to points, dates to
    day-month-year) while foreignisation governs identity (the company's own programme names stay in
    German with a first-mention gloss); the untranslatables get their five-escape decision each; and the
    quality pass runs the error taxonomy — accuracy, terminology, register — with numbers double-checked
    against the source tables (the single most common professional error class is transposed figures,
    not vocabulary). The brief, in short, converts taste into contract — which is what makes translation
    a profession rather than a talent.
  </p>

  <h2>Interpretation modes, in the room</h2>
  <p>
    Fill in the settings where each mode appears, because the mode chooses the preparation.
    <strong>Consecutive</strong> rules courtrooms, medical consultations, business negotiations —
    anywhere accuracy outranks speed and the speaker can pause; the interpreter's notes are a private
    shorthand (symbols for "therefore," arrows for causation, verticality for structure) reconstructing
    sense, not transcription. <strong>Simultaneous</strong> owns conferences and the booths of
    multilateral institutions (the EU's interpreting service is the world's largest employer of the
    craft); here the skills are anticipation (guessing the sentence's end from its genre and grammar),
    <strong>deverbalisation</strong> (dropping the source words to carry only the meaning into the other
    language — the skill that separates interpreters from fast transcoders), and numbers discipline
    (write every figure; memory fails exactly there). <strong>Chuchotage</strong> (whispered
    simultaneous for one or two listeners) and <strong>relay</strong> (interpreting through a third
    language when no direct pair exists — with its compounding error risk) complete the map. Each mode
    trains differently: consecutive rewards memory and composure; simultaneous rewards split attention
    and prediction — which is why interpreters specialise, and why the good ones make it look like
    translation with the time removed.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three research programmes round out the picture. <strong>Translation universals</strong>: corpus studies
    find consistent statistical fingerprints of translated language — simplification, explicitation,
    convergence, "translationese" — showing translation itself is a register, which style-sensitive clients
    now ask translators to avoid. <strong>Cognitive translation studies</strong> uses eye-tracking and
    keystroke logging to watch the process: pause patterns reveal where the real decisions live (usually
    not at hard words, but at sentences where two valid structures compete). <strong>Interpreter aptitude
    research</strong> asks what predicts success — working memory, yes, but the stronger predictors are
    extraction-of-sense speed and stress regulation, which reshaped selection tests. Open questions: can
    models learn the skopos (purpose-conditioned MT is an active research area), will low-resource languages
    — the majority of the world's thousands — ever get the data pipeline high-resource pairs enjoy, and as
    speech-to-speech interpreting AI reaches usable quality, which parts of the interpreter's oath
    (confidentiality, neutrality, presence) transfer to machines? What will not change is the wall the whole
    lesson is built on: translation is decision, and decisions need a decider who can be asked why.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Nida, <em>Toward a Science of Translating</em> (1964); Vermeer (1978); Venuti, <em>The Translator's Invisibility</em> (1995); Gile, <em>Basic Concepts and Models for Interpreter and Translator Training</em>; Rozan, <em>La prise de notes en interprétation consécutive</em> (1956).</p>
</TopicPage>
